proposed_fix:
1. Implement the lock with atomic SQL through $wpdb, bypassing add_option/update_option/delete_option and all option caches:
   - acquire: INSERT IGNORE (MySQL) / INSERT OR IGNORE (SQLite), or INSERT ... WHERE NOT EXISTS, succeeding only when rows_affected = 1;
   - stale takeover: UPDATE ... SET option_value = new WHERE option_name = LOCK AND option_value = <the stale value read>;
   - refresh: UPDATE ... WHERE option_value = <own current value>;
   - release: DELETE ... WHERE option_value = <own value>;
   - then clear the option caches, including notoptions.
2. In step(), read the job directly from the DB after acquiring the lock.
3. Keep a job version and write the job with a compare-and-swap on token+version.
4. Check refresh_lock() before every item, and on failure stop without saving and never finish a move. Do the same in Hosting::sync.
5. Serialize preview/start/cancel with the same lock or a job-version CAS, so a preview cannot replace a job started in between.
6. Make the 'failed' path re-check a fresh status and token.
7. Before creating an episode, re-query the GUID with a fresh DB query, and ideally hold a short per-GUID lock, so duplicates are impossible even if the job lock fails.
8. Compute counters from what the running process added to the fresh job, never from a stale snapshot.

